 conference last year at  the Vatican and the most moving thing  happened Larry Summers who used to be  president of Harvard when I was an  undergrad he did various things that  really pissed us off he like opposed the  student living wage campaign there was a  big student campaign to get contract  workers who were not directly employed  by Harvard paid living wages and he  opposed it and he said on stage at the  Vatican but that was his biggest mistake  as a university president he said I now  realize that the biggest power I had was  with the capital that we had as a  university and in our supply chain and  that if we could leverage the money that  we were spending as a big employer  including of contract workers to do more  good in the community that's probably  the best thing that Harvard could do for  the region that we're in and to hear  Larry Summers an economist say that an  economists who used to oppose all these  super hippie campaigns by the student  say that on stage in front of the Pope  it was really inspiring and hopeful to  me and so I think the people who direct  these big pools of capital like  university endowments are starting to  come around and that's it's really  hopeful to me  the question the question is do we see  the summer school concept expanding into  other countries and do we see an  opportunity to expand our work into  other countries so as you can probably  tell from the three things four things  if you include the book that I told you  about my problem is an entrepreneur is  like constantly being you know pulled in  two different directions and the problem  is a social entrepreneur is that you  constantly feel like if you don't do  that you're hurting people right like  you're depriving people of the chance to  move out of poverty if you don't move  into a new region so it feels very hard  to make those calls and to be focused  what we decided to do as a company was  to expand not through direct service  offering so rather than set 